;^) You have a humorous way of making your point.  However, Mjollner is
a bit much for a first level character anyway, don't you think?  Otherwise
why don't we just give everyone named weapons in their starting inventory?

>     Don't get me wrong.  I love the element of risk in Nethack.  I
>don't like the idea that a benevolent gift from one's God turns into a
>potential death trap.

OK, as I mentioned, I think that telepathy + blindfold is absolutely
necessary before considering entering the fateful level.  And teleportitous
(with control) is recommended.  I am sort of looking at it as a quest
that your diety has *sent* you on.  You don't choose the weapon, right?

You receive a crystal ball and instructions: "I send you on a quest, to
retrieve the mighty Stormbringer; this crystal ball will be your guide;
this ring will help you in time of trouble... (ring of teleport control)
...beware if another would wield the weapon first,.. etc." 

>     I wouldn't mind if the box was guarded, by the way.  Creating a
>vault seems like too much coding...

Well, maybe I don't appreciate the intricacies involved, but it seems to
me that there are already a number of ways in which an existing level can
have its layout changed, so I don't really understand why adding a vault
would be too difficult to attempt.


>                              ...But it couldn't be too much trouble
>to map out a future level and create a box and a reasonably difficult
>monster horde (something that has a chance of appearing on the level
>anyway); this could be called a "quest" of a minor variety, and would
>present a much more workable alternative to creating a virtually
>undefeatable (at hand-to-hand) single monster.

This is a workable alternative.  Perhaps the box would be in the center of a
treasure zoo.  I like the added risk of having the weapon out on the ground.
That is just my own preference.  I would suggest in particular that if
Stormbringer is wielded by a monster and drains some of your levels, but
you succeed in destroying the monster and retrieving the weapon, then you
should regain the lost levels while wielding Stormbringer (if my scheme
were implemented).  You would want to wield Stormbringer as much as possible
from then on, however, since your lost experience points would be captive
inside of Stormbringer.  There might be a use for the magician-in-the-mall
here.  He might know a special spell that could transfer your experience
points back into your person from Stormbringer (yes, I know, too complex.
Well, I like complexity, it makes things interesting).

>     I'm not objecting to your scheme as applied to wishes.  But (unless
>your God is going to grant you a crystal ball first before handing
>enemies implements of death and destruction) divine gifts are a
>different matter.  Besides, all the crystal ball would you is whether
>you're on the right level or not; it's not all that big a help.

If the weapon is in a vault then magic mapping will reveal its location
unerringly.  Maybe that should be included in your quest-equipment?
OK, here's my list of equipment for the quest:

crystal ball
ring of teleport control
scroll of magic mapping
blindfold? (giving away too much? - does the danger involved permit it?)

+ instructions to use the equipment wisely (hint,hint; nudge,nudge).

>     Great-- I just have some disagreements on the implementation.  I
>note that Nethack has added in some intermediate goals, although the
>Tower seems to be one of the bigger flops as far as that's concerned (no
>motivation).

Yes, I agree.  Some points:

o  95%+ of the time you would find the level, read the scroll, teleport
   to the weapon and retrieve it, no problem.

o  The < 5% of the time when something got into the vault first (I
   would say orcs or killer bees are most likely) you would have to be
   clever to get your weapon.  You would possibly need to get some more
   equipment (on other levels of course) before returning and making the
   attempt.

o  I don't have a problem with occassionally dying in the attempt.
   Obviously that character did not prove to be worthy to wield the
   weapon he was sent questing for.

o  While it would be horrible when it happened, imagine the epic tale
   of woe when you recount the time when the dwarf king came after
   you armed with Mjollner.  The obituary should mention that the
   dwarf king was wielding Mjollner and you were on a quest.

We disagree on some things, and I have no problem with that.  But I
think it would actually be more fun this way (my opinion).

